**must fix** — This `ResizeObserver` never attaches on the cold-load path, so neither behaviour the comment above promises actually happens.

`useEffect(..., [])` runs on the first commit, but the page early-returns `<PerpsDetailPageSkeleton />` while `marketsLoading` is true (line 1740). At that point `bodyRef.current` is `null`, the effect returns early — and because the dependency array is empty, it never retries once the real body mounts.

Reproduced against the running extension (cold reload, order book opened, then viewport changed with no user interaction):

```
wide 1100px, book opened             | aria-valuenow=33 aria-valuemax=60
viewport 1100 -> 700, no interaction | aria-valuenow=33 aria-valuemax=60   <- observer never fires
viewport 700 -> 360, no interaction  | aria-valuenow=33 aria-valuemax=60   <- no re-clamp, body overflows
after ONE ArrowRight keypress        | aria-valuenow=31 aria-valuemax=35   <- keydown handler fixes it
```

For contrast, entering the page by SPA navigation from perps home (markets already cached, so the body renders on the first commit) gives the correct `aria-valuemax=35` immediately — the bug is entry-path dependent.

So, until the user touches the divider: assistive tech is told the split can reach 60% when the reachable ceiling at 360px is ~35%, and a width chosen on a wide body is not re-clamped when the container narrows — exactly the two things the comment says this effect prevents. `handleOrderBookResizeStart` / `handleOrderBookResizeKeyDown` read `bodyRef` at event time, which is why one keypress silently repairs it.

A callback ref runs setup whenever the node actually mounts:

```tsx
const [bodyEl, setBodyEl] = useState<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);
// then: <div ref={setBodyEl} …>
useEffect(() => {
  if (!bodyEl || typeof ResizeObserver === 'undefined') {
    return undefined;
  }
  const observer = new ResizeObserver(() => {
    const { width } = bodyEl.getBoundingClientRect();
    const maxPct = getOrderBookMaxWidthPct(width);
    setOrderBookMaxWidthPct(maxPct);
    setOrderBookWidthPct((pct) => clampOrderBookWidthPct(pct, width));
  });
  observer.observe(bodyEl);
  return () => observer.disconnect();
}, [bodyEl]);
```

Worth noting the existing `exposes the pixel-aware width ceiling on aria-valuemax for a 360px popup` test cannot fail on this: it renders with markets already loaded, so `bodyRef` is populated on the first commit. A test that starts with `marketsLoading: true` and then flips it would cover the real path.

**suggestion** — `preventDefault()` on `mousedown` also suppresses the browser's default focus behaviour, so the divider never takes focus when it is clicked or dragged.

I checked this with a real (trusted) CDP mouse click rather than a synthetic one, to be sure it wasn't an automation artifact — focus stays on whatever was focused before (`TRUSTED_CLICK_FOCUS=perps-order-book-toggle`). The separator has `tabIndex={0}`, so it is still reachable with Tab and the keyboard resizing works; the gap is that a mouse user who drags the divider then cannot fine-tune with Arrow keys without tabbing back to it, which is the natural follow-up gesture.

The `preventDefault()` itself is doing useful work (it stops text selection during the drag), so the usual pairing is to keep it and focus explicitly:

```tsx
const handleOrderBookResizeStart = useCallback(
  (event: React.MouseEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    event.currentTarget.focus();
    setIsResizingOrderBook(true);
  },
  [],
);
```
